        "original_id": "c1d070cb-8611-55c8-ba9e-d9e0d85c95be",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lucy Maud Montgomery, Anne Shirley\n- Works: Anne of Ingleside, Anne of Green Gables, Road to Avonlea\n- Companies: McClelland and Stewart, Frederick A. Stokes Company\n- Places: Avonlea, Prince Edward Island, Toronto, New York, Canada\n- Concepts: fictional communities, children's novels\n\nFacts:\n- Lucy Maud Montgomery was a Canadian author born on November 30, 1874, and she published as L.M. Montgomery.\n- In 1908, Lucy Maud Montgomery began her best-known novel series with Anne of Green Gables.\n- Anne Shirley is the orphaned central character of Anne of Green Gables, and the character made Lucy Maud Montgomery famous in her lifetime and gave her an international following.\n- Avonlea is a fictional community on Prince Edward Island, Canada, and it is the setting of Anne of Green Gables, its sequels, and the television series Road to Avonlea.\n- Anne of Ingleside is a children's novel by Canadian author Lucy Maud Montgomery.\n- Anne of Ingleside was first published in July 1939 by McClelland and Stewart in Toronto and by the Frederick A. Stokes Company in New York.\n- Lucy Maud Montgomery died on April 24, 1942.",
